.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBmbtrnpp\fP is a command line program, normally run unattended aboard an
autonomous underwater vehicle (AUV) or other survey platform, that reads
multibeam bathymetry in real time (from a datalist, a single swath data
file, or a live network socket), applies automated cleaning, swath-width
limiting, and sounding decimation, and then passes the resulting bathymetry
soundings on to a terrain relative navigation (TRN) engine embedded in the
program. \fBmbtrnpp\fP is one of the \fBMB-System\fP utilities in the
\fBmbtrnutils\fP source directory; it is an optional program, built only
when \fBMB-System\fP is configured with TRN support enabled
(\fB--enable-mbtrn\fP with autotools, or \fB-DbuildTRN=ON\fP with
\fBCMake\fP, both of which are the default), and further requires TRN
support (\fB--enable-mbtnav\fP or \fB-DbuildTRNLCM\fP-related \fBCMake\fP
options) to actually run TRN rather than simply relaying bathymetry.

\fBmbtrnpp\fP has no interactive display; it is configured entirely through
command line options and, optionally, a configuration file specified with
\fB--config\fP. The order of precedence for setting an option is (highest to
lowest) command line options, configuration file options, and compiled-in
defaults. Because \fBmbtrnpp\fP is normally run unattended and continuously,
most deployments use a shell wrapper that first sources an environment file
(setting variables such as \fBTRN_HOST\fP, \fBTRN_MAPFILES\fP,
\fBTRN_DATAFILES\fP, and \fBTRN_LOGFILES\fP that are referenced by mnemonic
in the configuration file and by \fBmbtrnpp\fP itself), and then invokes
\fBmbtrnpp\fP with \fB--config\fP pointing to a configuration file tailored
to the vehicle and mission. The \fB--help\fP option is useful even in normal
operation, since \fBmbtrnpp\fP prints its default, configuration-file, and
final (command-line-overridden) settings to stderr as it starts up, before
exiting without reading any data; this allows a configuration to be checked
without actually running a survey.

Input bathymetry may come from a recursive \fBMB-System\fP datalist or a
single swath data file (in which case \fB--format\fP normally must also be
given), read exactly as with other \fBMB-System\fP utilities, or from a live
data socket (e.g. a Reson/Teledyne multibeam sonar or 7k Center, specified
with an \fB--input\fP value beginning \fIsocket:\fP) for real-time
operation. As pings are read, \fBmbtrnpp\fP applies an automated median
filter across and along track to flag anomalous soundings, limits the swath
to the width set by \fB--swath-width\fP, and decimates each ping to the
number of soundings set by \fB--soundings\fP. The processed soundings are
packaged into MB1 records, which may be logged to a binary file, served to
network clients, and/or passed directly to the embedded TRN instance for
terrain-relative position estimation (when TRN is enabled with
\fB--trn-en\fP and \fBMB-System\fP was built with TRN navigation support).
TRN position estimates and updates may in turn be logged and/or served to
network clients using the \fB--trn-out\fP option.

\fBmbtrnpp\fP writes several kinds of log files (an MB-System processing
log, an mbtrnpp session log, MB1 server and TRN server/update session logs,
the MB1 binary record log, and, if TRN is enabled, a TRN instance log
directory) into the directory set by \fB--log-directory\fP; a symbolic link
named \fBmbtrnpp-latest\fP pointing at this directory is created (or
replaced) each time \fBmbtrnpp\fP starts. See the \fBREADME-mbtrnpp.md\fP
file distributed with \fBMB-System\fP in \fBsrc/mbtrn/tools/cfg\fP for a
complete description of the environment and configuration files, the
network output specifiers, log file naming, and troubleshooting tips.

