.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \--verbose
This option increases the verbosity of \fBmbtrnpp\fP, which means that more
information than by default is output to the stderr stream of the shell.
Negative values (e.g. \fB--verbose=-2\fP) also select the printing of the
program name and \fBMB-System\fP version banner at startup.
.TP
.B \--help
This option causes \fBmbtrnpp\fP to print a description of what it does and
a summary of the command line options, and then exit without reading any
data. Because \fBmbtrnpp\fP always prints its default, configuration-file,
and final settings to stderr before checking for \fB--help\fP, this option
is a convenient way to verify a configuration (including one loaded with
\fB--config\fP) without starting a survey.
.TP
.B \--config=path
This option specifies a configuration file containing any of the
\fBmbtrnpp\fP command line options, one \fIkey\fP\fB=\fP\fIvalue\fP pair
per line (using the same option names as the command line, without the
leading \fB--\fP). Configuration file options override the compiled-in
defaults, and are in turn overridden by the same option given explicitly on
the command line. The \fB--config\fP option itself is recognized before any
other command line processing, so a configuration file may be combined with
command-line overrides in a single invocation. See the annotated example
configuration file \fBsrc/mbtrn/tools/cfg/mbtrnpp-cfg.example\fP distributed
with \fBMB-System\fP.
.TP
.B \--log-directory=path
This option sets the directory into which \fBmbtrnpp\fP writes its log
files (the \fBMB-System\fP processing log, the mbtrnpp and MB1/TRN server
session logs, the MB1 binary record log, and, if TRN is enabled, the TRN
instance log directory). The directory is created if it does not already
exist. The default is the current working directory (\fB.\fP). A symbolic
link named \fBmbtrnpp-latest\fP pointing at this directory is created (or
replaced) each time \fBmbtrnpp\fP starts.
.TP
.B \--input=datalist|file|socket_definition|serial_definition
This option specifies the source of input bathymetry data, exactly as the
\fB--input\fP (\fB-I\fP) option does in other \fBMB-System\fP utilities,
with the addition of live socket and serial input. If the value does not
begin with \fIsocket:\fP or \fIserial:\fP, it is read as an \fBMB-System\fP
recursive datalist (in which case \fB--format\fP is not required, since each
listed file names its own format) or as a single swath data file (in which
case \fB--format\fP must also be given). If the value begins \fIsocket:\fP,
the remainder is a socket definition string (e.g. identifying a
Reson/Teledyne sonar or 7k Center host and port) used to read multibeam
pings from a live network connection instead of a file. If the value begins
\fIserial:\fP, the remainder is a serial port definition string used to read
multibeam pings from a live serial connection instead of a file. In either
live case, \fB--format\fP must be set to the format ID appropriate for the
data stream. The default input is \fBstdin\fP.
.TP
.B \--output=file|socket
This option enables output of processed MB1 records to a binary file
and/or a network socket server. A value of \fIfile\fP enables logging to a
file with the default \fBMB-System\fP-generated name; a value of the form
\fIfile:\fP\fIpath\fP logs to \fIpath\fP instead. A value of \fIsocket\fP
enables the MB1 socket server using its default host and port; a value of
the form \fIsocket:\fP\fIhost\fP\fB:\fP\fIport\fP overrides the host and/or
port. Multiple comma-separated values may be given. This option predates,
and is functionally a subset of, the more general \fB--mb-out\fP option;
new configurations should prefer \fB--mb-out\fP.
.TP
.B \--format=format
This option sets the \fBMB-System\fP format ID of the input data, exactly
as the \fB--format\fP (\fB-F\fP) option does in other \fBMB-System\fP
utilities. It is required when \fB--input\fP names a single file or a live
socket; it is not needed when \fB--input\fP names a datalist, since each
file listed in the datalist specifies its own format.
.TP
.B \--platform-file=file
This option specifies an \fBMB-System\fP platform definition file
describing the sensor offsets and orientation of the survey platform
(vehicle), exactly as used by \fBmbpreprocess\fP and other \fBMB-System\fP
utilities that support platform files. See the \fBMB-System\fP manual page
for a description of the platform file format.
.TP
.B \--platform-target-sensor=sensor_id
This option selects, by ID, the sensor within the platform file (given by
\fB--platform-file\fP) whose offsets are applied to the bathymetric
soundings.
.TP
.B \--tide-model=file
This option specifies a tide model file used to remove tidal variation from
the input bathymetry, following the same tide model file format used by
\fBmbotps\fP and \fBmbprocess\fP elsewhere in \fBMB-System\fP.
.TP
.B \--swath-width=value
This option sets the width of the sonar swath, in decimal degrees measured
from nadir, that is retained from each ping; soundings outside this swath
are excluded from output. The default is 90.0 (the full swath).
\fB--swath\fP is accepted as an equivalent, shorter alias for this option.
.TP
.B \--soundings=value
This option sets the number of soundings output per ping after decimation.
Soundings are selected evenly across the retained swath. The default is 11.
.TP
.B \--median-filter=threshold/nx/ny
This option enables an automated median filter applied to the bathymetry
before decimation, and sets its parameters: \fIthreshold\fP is the fractional
deviation from the local median depth (relative to depth) beyond which a
sounding is flagged bad, and \fInx\fP and \fIny\fP are respectively the
number of soundings across track and the number of pings along track
included in the local neighborhood used to compute the median. If this
option is not given, the median filter is disabled.
.TP
.B \--statsec=d.d
This option sets the interval, in seconds, at which \fBmbtrnpp\fP logs
periodic processing statistics (selected by \fB--statflags\fP).
.TP
.B \--statflags=<MSF_STATUS|MSF_EVENT|MSF_ASTAT|MSF_PSTAT|MSF_READER>
This option selects which categories of processing statistics
\fBmbtrnpp\fP logs, as a \fB|\fP- (or \fB:\fP-) delimited list of flag
names: \fIMSF_STATUS\fP enables status counters, \fIMSF_EVENT\fP enables
event/error counters, \fIMSF_ASTAT\fP enables aggregate (cumulative)
statistics, \fIMSF_PSTAT\fP enables periodic (interval) statistics, and
\fIMSF_READER\fP enables statistics from the socket data reader. The
default is \fIMSF_STATUS|MSF_EVENT|MSF_ASTAT|MSF_PSTAT\fP.
.TP
.B \--delay=n
This option sets a delay, in milliseconds, added to the main MB1 processing
loop on each iteration; it may be used to throttle processing when input
data arrive faster than downstream consumers (TRN, network clients) can
keep up. The default is 0 (no delay).
.TP
.B \--mb-out=mb1svr[:host:port]/mb1/file[:path]/reson/nomb1/noreson/nombsvr/nombtrnpp
This option configures MB1 (processed bathymetry) output, as a
\fB,\fP-delimited list of tokens: \fImb1svr\fP (optionally followed by
\fB:\fP\fIhost\fP\fB:\fP\fIport\fP) enables the MB1 network server;
\fImb1\fP enables binary MB1 record logging using the default log file
name; \fIfile\fP (optionally followed by \fB:\fP\fIpath\fP) enables binary
MB1 record logging, to \fIpath\fP if given; \fIreson\fP enables logging of
the raw Reson/Teledyne S7K data frames; and the \fIno\fP-prefixed tokens
(\fInomb1\fP, \fInoreson\fP, \fInombsvr\fP, \fInombtrnpp\fP) disable the
corresponding output (\fInombtrnpp\fP disables the mbtrnpp message log
itself, which is not recommended). Tokens are processed in order, so a
later token can override an earlier one on the same command line.
.TP
.B \--mbhbn=n
This option sets the number of missed heartbeats the MB1 server tolerates
from a client before dropping the connection.
.TP
.B \--mbhbt=d.d
This option sets the heartbeat timeout, in seconds, for the MB1 server.
.TP
.B \--trn-en
This option enables terrain relative navigation processing using the
embedded TRN instance; \fBmbtrnpp\fP must have been built with TRN
navigation support (\fB--enable-mbtnav\fP, or the corresponding \fBCMake\fP
option) for TRN processing to actually occur. When disabled, \fBmbtrnpp\fP
still reads, cleans, and outputs bathymetry, but does not attempt terrain
navigation. TRN is enabled by default; use \fB--trn-en=N\fP (or \fB=0\fP) to
disable it.
.TP
.B \--trn-dev=s
This option selects the sonar device type used to interpret live socket
input, by mnemonic (e.g. \fI7125_200\fP for a Seabat 7125 at 200 kHz,
\fI7125_400\fP for a Seabat 7125 at 400 kHz, or \fIT50\fP for a T50-S/T50-R).
The mnemonic is translated internally to the device and system enumerator
values required to subscribe to the sonar's data stream; see \fBr7kc.h\fP in
the \fBMB-System\fP source for the full mapping.
.TP
.B \--use-proj[=bool]
This option enables (the default) or disables use of the \fBPROJ\fP
cartographic projection library to convert geographic coordinates to the
projected coordinate system used internally by TRN, instead of a simple UTM
calculation.
.TP
.B \--projection=projection_id
This option sets the numeric \fBPROJ\fP projection ID used when
\fB--use-proj\fP is enabled.
.TP
.B \--trn-crs=s
This option sets the \fBPROJ\fP coordinate reference system string used
when \fB--use-proj\fP is enabled, in place of \fB--projection\fP.
.TP
.B \--trn-utm=zone
This option sets the UTM zone (an integer from 1 to 60) used for TRN
processing when \fB--use-proj\fP is disabled (e.g. zone 9 for the axial
seamount test site, zone 10 for Monterey Bay).
.TP
.B \--trn-map=path
This option specifies the path to the terrain reference map used by the TRN
server; it may be a single map file or a directory of tiled map files. A
terrain map is required for TRN processing to converge.
.TP
.B \--trn-cfg=path
This option specifies the path to the TRN configuration file, which sets
per-vehicle TRN filter and sensor specification parameters. A TRN
configuration file is required for TRN processing.
.TP
.B \--trn-par=path
This option specifies the path to a TRN particle filter configuration file,
used only when the TRN particle filter is selected (see \fB--trn-ftype\fP).
.TP
.B \--trn-mid=id
This option sets the TRN mission ID string used in naming the TRN server's
own log files and log directory.
.TP
.B \--trn-mtype=n
This option sets the TRN terrain map type: \fI1\fP selects a DEM
(elevation grid) map, and \fI2\fP selects a bottom-object (BO) map.
.TP
.B \--trn-sensor-type=n
This option sets the TRN sensor type identifying the source of bathymetry
supplied to TRN; the default corresponds to a generic \fBMB-System\fP
(\fBMB1\fP) multibeam source.
.TP
.B \--trn-ftype=n
This option sets the TRN estimation filter type: \fI0\fP disables
filtering, \fI1\fP selects a point-mass filter, \fI2\fP selects a particle
filter, and \fI3\fP selects a filter bank.
.TP
.B \--trn-fgrade=n
This option sets the TRN filter grade: \fI0\fP selects a low-grade filter
configuration, and \fI1\fP (the default) selects a high-grade filter
configuration.
.TP
.B \--trn-freinit=n
This option enables (\fI1\fP, the default) or disables (\fI0\fP) automatic
reinitialization of the TRN estimation filter, e.g. after divergence.
.TP
.B \--trn-mweight=n
This option selects the TRN modified-weighting scheme used during
filtering: \fI0\fP applies no weighting modification, \fI1\fP applies the
original alpha modification, \fI2\fP applies crossbeam weighting with the
alpha modification, \fI3\fP applies subcloud weighting with the original
alpha modification, and \fI4\fP (the default) applies subcloud weighting
with modified NIS (normalized innovation squared) always enabled.
.TP
.B \--trn-ncov=value
This option sets the TRN convergence criterion for northing covariance; a
TRN solution is not considered converged unless its northing covariance is
below this value.
.TP
.B \--trn-nerr=value
This option sets the TRN convergence criterion for northing error.
.TP
.B \--trn-ecov=value
This option sets the TRN convergence criterion for easting covariance.
.TP
.B \--trn-eerr=value
This option sets the TRN convergence criterion for easting error.
.TP
.B \--trn-decn=n
This option sets the TRN update decimation modulus: a TRN position update
is computed and issued only for every \fIn\fPth processed MB1 sounding
record. The default is 0 (update on every record).
.TP
.B \--trn-decs=d.d
This option sets a minimum time interval, in seconds, between TRN position
updates, as a decimation criterion alternative (or in addition) to
\fB--trn-decn\fP.
.TP
.B \--covariance-magnitude-max=value
This option sets the maximum TRN covariance magnitude below which a TRN
solution is considered a candidate for stable convergence; smaller values
impose a stricter convergence requirement. A value around 10.0 is typical
for 1-meter-scale mapping.
.TP
.B \--convergence-repeat-min=n
This option sets the number of consecutive TRN update cycles that must meet
the convergence criteria (\fB--covariance-magnitude-max\fP and related
limits) before the TRN solution is declared stably converged. A value
around 400 is typical for 1-meter-scale mapping.
.TP
.B \--reinit-search=reinit_search_xy/reinit_search_z
This option sets the size, in meters, of the horizontal
(\fIreinit_search_xy\fP) and vertical (\fIreinit_search_z\fP) search area
used by TRN when reinitializing its position estimate. The defaults are 60
m horizontal and 5 m vertical.
.TP
.B \--reinit-gain[=bool]
This option enables or disables gating TRN reinitialization using the
sonar's transmit gain setting (e.g. to avoid reinitializing on pings taken
with an atypical gain). Disabled by default.
.TP
.B \--reinit-file[=bool]
This option enables or disables reinitializing TRN whenever a new input
file begins while reading from a datalist. Disabled by default; only
meaningful when reading from a datalist or file rather than a live socket.
.TP
.B \--reinit-xyoffset=xyoffset_max
This option enables reinitialization of TRN whenever the magnitude of its
converged horizontal (xy) offset from the vehicle's dead-reckoned position
exceeds \fIxyoffset_max\fP, in meters.
.TP
.B \--reinit-zoffset=zoffset_min/zoffset_max
This option enables reinitialization of TRN whenever the magnitude of its
converged vertical (z) offset falls below \fIzoffset_min\fP or exceeds
\fIzoffset_max\fP, in meters.
.TP
.B \--random-offset
This option enables injection of a randomized offset into the TRN filter's
initial position estimate; this is intended for testing TRN's ability to
reconverge after reinitialization, and should not normally be used during
an actual survey.
.TP
.B \--trn-out=trnsvr[:host:port]/trnusvr[:host:port]/trnumsvr[:group:port:ttl]/trnu/trnub/sout/serr/debug/notrnsvr/notrnusvr/notrnumsvr
This option configures TRN position/update output, as a \fB,\fP-delimited
list of tokens: \fItrnsvr\fP (optionally followed by
\fB:\fP\fIhost\fP\fB:\fP\fIport\fP) enables the TRN TCP server interface;
\fItrnusvr\fP (optionally followed by \fB:\fP\fIhost\fP\fB:\fP\fIport\fP)
enables the TRN update (TRNU) TCP server; \fItrnumsvr\fP (optionally
followed by \fB:\fP\fIgroup\fP\fB:\fP\fIport\fP\fB:\fP\fIttl\fP) enables a
TRN update UDP multicast server; \fItrnu\fP enables ASCII logging of TRN
update records, and \fItrnub\fP enables binary logging of the same;
\fIsout\fP and \fIserr\fP send TRN output to stdout and stderr,
respectively; \fIdebug\fP enables verbose TRN debug output; and the
\fIno\fP-prefixed tokens (\fInotrnsvr\fP, \fInotrnusvr\fP, \fInotrnumsvr\fP)
disable the corresponding server. The mnemonics \fBTRN_HOST\fP and
\fBTRN_GROUP\fP may be used in place of an explicit \fIhost\fP or
\fIgroup\fP, in which case the corresponding environment variable is
substituted at run time.
.TP
.B \--trnhbt=d.d
This option sets the heartbeat timeout, in seconds, for the TRN TCP server.
.TP
.B \--trnuhbt=d.d
This option sets the heartbeat timeout, in seconds, for the TRN update
(TRNU) TCP server.

.SH EXAMPLES
Suppose you want to check a mission configuration before running a survey,
without actually starting to process data. Sourcing the mission's
environment file first makes its mnemonic variables (\fBTRN_HOST\fP,
\fBTRN_MAPFILES\fP, and so on) available for substitution in the
configuration file:
.br
    . env/mbtrnpp-Sentry.env
.br
    mbtrnpp --config=TRNcfg/mbtrnpp-Sentry.cfg --help
.br
\fBmbtrnpp\fP prints its default settings, the settings loaded from the
configuration file, and the final effective settings to stderr, then exits
without reading any data.

To actually run the same configuration as a real-time TRN preprocessor,
logging to a mission-specific log directory:
.br
    . env/mbtrnpp-Sentry.env
.br
    mbtrnpp --config=TRNcfg/mbtrnpp-Sentry.cfg \\
.br
            --log-directory=/home/mappingauv/logs/mbtrnpp

To process a single existing swath file offline (e.g. to test cleaning and
TRN convergence against a previously recorded survey) rather than a live
sonar socket, overriding the configuration file's \fB--input\fP setting:
.br
    mbtrnpp --config=TRNcfg/mbtrnpp-Sentry.cfg \\
.br
            --input=20240510_120000.mb88 --format=88

